/** \page threading Threading Model

As of Zorba 5.0, the engine is <b>not thread-safe</b>. Use it from one thread.

\section threading_rule The rule

- <b>One engine per process.</b> Zorba::getInstance() returns a process-wide
  singleton that sits on a single global store. There is no way to create a
  second, independent engine or store in the same process, so "one engine per
  thread" is not possible inside one process.
- <b>Use the engine from one thread at a time.</b> This covers the
  Zorba object and everything created from it: XQuery objects, contexts,
  iterators, Item and ItemFactory, the XmlDataManager and JsonDataManager,
  and the collections and documents in the store. Do not call any of them
  concurrently from several threads, not even on different XQuery objects,
  because they all share the same store.
- If an application must call Zorba from several threads, serialize <i>all</i>
  Zorba calls (including destruction of items and queries, and iteration over
  results) with one mutex of your own. Zorba itself does not guarantee
  this arrangement, and it is not covered by tests.
- <b>Separate processes are safe.</b> To run queries in parallel, run one
  process per worker.

\section threading_why Why

The default build sets the CMake option ZORBA_FOR_ONE_THREAD_ONLY to ON. This
compiles out all engine locks (the SYNC_CODE macro) and makes reference counts
plain, non-atomic integers. ThreadSanitizer reports data races in the store
item reference counts, the QName pool, plan iterators and rchandle when
several threads share the engine.

The one exception is decimal arithmetic: the state of the arbitrary-precision
library (MAPM) behind xs:decimal, and xs:integer in builds with
ZORBA_WITH_BIG_INTEGER, is per thread. That removes one source of races but
does not make the engine thread-safe.

\section threading_off ZORBA_FOR_ONE_THREAD_ONLY=OFF

Configuring with -DZORBA_FOR_ONE_THREAD_ONLY=OFF compiles the engine locks
in. That build is <b>unsupported</b>: it is not ThreadSanitizer-clean, and the
rule above still applies.

\section threading_future Future

Making the engine thread-safe is planned for a 5.x release, before the C API
promises multithreading. Until then, treat anything beyond the rule above as
undefined behavior.

*/
